"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, and lack of lymphovascular invasion. The preoperative PSA was 4.8, and the surgical pathology showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with a pathological T2 stage and negative margins. Conversely, the absence of lymph node removal means nodal status is unknown, which widens uncertainty. The biopsy showed a lower grade (Gleason 3+3, ISUP grade group 1) than the prostatectomy specimen. The missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of local control. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical unknowns that would significantly revise this prognosis. The current horizon suggests a follow-up period within the heuristic sensitivity range. The fixed estimated horizon is 81.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 53.15–110.38 months; its coverage has not been validated."
